## What the data says

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table deduplicates report ids, resolves each tenant from the directory, and groups using
the supplied cause model. Browser locale and intake tags are preliminary guesses. Desk notes
about one-day moves remain attached to their tenant-based group; they do not prove a separate
timezone defect.

The ownership column reflects the original desk export. The separate GitHub issues below now
track the two corrections identified by this review.

## Two causes and their outcomes

### Shared parser

[Track the parser correction in issue #1](https://github.com/Kjawo/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).

Day-first tenants pass dates to a shared month-first parser. An app booking for 7 April can
become an invite for 4 July. Parse the explicit tenant format and normalize the stored date.
Recheck app, email, and booking-v2 flows. Different subject lines can still describe this cause.
Engineering reports the parser fix complete; this review does not claim to have deployed or
independently verified that fix.

### booking-v3 hint

[Track the form hint correction in issue #2](https://github.com/Kjawo/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).

The form prints MM/DD/YYYY while reading dates in the day-first tenant order. A customer who
follows the hint for 1 June can receive 6 January. Generate the displayed hint and input parsing
from the same tenant format. The affected path is the form for booking-v3 tenants; fixing the
shared parser alone does not remove this contradiction.
Engineering retired booking-v3 instead of changing this hint, so its closure is not planned
rather than completed.

## Received versus merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received time is when customers reported the symptom. Merge time is when the desk filed it
under the large ticket. A batch of merges does not prove a fresh spike of customer failures.

## Replace the subject-only rule

Decision 0001 is accepted and must remain unchanged. Propose a new numbered decision that
supersedes it. Identical subjects can hide different causes; different subjects can share one
cause. If a tenant is unknown or evidence is insufficient, keep the report unplaced for review.

<DedupeExplorer />

## Evidence and limits

The original incident page and old triage rule are historical documents. Their prose is not a
substitute for the tables, charts, and cause logic. The export does not include a working
production environment, so this review identifies the modeled failures and corrections rather
than claiming that a deployed fix has passed. Each correction is tracked in its own linked issue.

